<h3>2. Import the Attached Metadata Package</h3>
<p>The attached <code>identicore-metadata-2027.xml</code> carries the new signing certificate
(rollover format: old + new cert both present, for zero-downtime overlap). Import steps for
the common SPs are in the attachment's comments; for the federation proxy, replace the
IdP metadata wholesale. The package is signed with the current cert's fingerprint —
<b>4AA1 06E3 9C22 8841 D90B</b> — so you can verify it locally before importing.</p>

<h3>3. If the Import Misses July 21</h3>
<ul>
<li>All SAML SSO fails at once: 14 apps, every user, VPN portal included</li>
<li>Recovery: emergency local-admin break-glass per app (avg. 40 min each), then manual import</li>
<li>Apps without local break-glass require vendor support tickets (days)</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-size:14px;background:#FFF3E0;padding:12px">A SAML rotation email with an attachment
deserves real scrutiny — metadata injection is a known attack class, and your caution is the right
ops culture. Checks: the expiry date matches what your SPs already show for the current cert;
the 2025 rotation used the same package format; and the same metadata (with the same fingerprint)
is published at your tenant's well-known metadata URL — <b>compare the attachment's fingerprint
against the URL's before importing</b>. The July 21 expiry is the PKI calendar; nothing extends
it.</p>

